#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-revert(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:7
msgid "git-revert - Revert some existing commits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t'git revert' [--[no-]edit] [-n] [-m <parent-number>] [-s] [-S[<keyid>]] "
"<commit>...\n"
"\t'git revert' (--continue | --skip | --abort | --quit)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:21
msgid ""
"Given one or more existing commits, revert the changes that the related "
"patches introduce, and record some new commits that record them.  This "
"requires your working tree to be clean (no modifications from the HEAD "
"commit)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:30
msgid ""
"Note: 'git revert' is used to record some new commits to reverse the effect "
"of some earlier commits (often only a faulty one).  If you want to throw "
"away all uncommitted changes in your working directory, you should see "
"linkgit:git-reset[1], particularly the `--hard` option.  If you want to "
"extract specific files as they were in another commit, you should see "
"linkgit:git-restore[1], specifically the `--source` option. Take care with "
"these alternatives as both will discard uncommitted changes in your working "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:33
msgid ""
"See \"Reset, restore and revert\" in linkgit:git[1] for the differences "
"between the three commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "<commit>..."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:43
msgid ""
"Commits to revert.  For a more complete list of ways to spell commit names, "
"see linkgit:gitrevisions[7].  Sets of commits can also be given but no "
"traversal is done by default, see linkgit:git-rev-list[1] and its "
"`--no-walk` option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-e::`\n"
"`--edit::`\n"
"\tWith this option, 'git revert' will let you edit the commit\n"
"\tmessage prior to committing the revert. This is the default if\n"
"\tyou run the command from a terminal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-m`\n"
"`--mainline`\n"
"\tUsually you cannot revert a merge because you do not know which\n"
"\tside of the merge should be considered the mainline.  This\n"
"\toption specifies the parent number (starting from 1) of\n"
"\tthe mainline and allows revert to reverse the change\n"
"\trelative to the specified parent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:62
msgid ""
"Reverting a merge commit declares that you will never want the tree changes "
"brought in by the merge.  As a result, later merges will only bring in tree "
"changes introduced by commits that are not ancestors of the previously "
"reverted merge.  This may or may not be what you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:65
msgid ""
"See the link:howto/revert-a-faulty-merge.html[revert-a-faulty-merge How-To] "
"for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-edit::`\n"
"\tWith this option, 'git revert' will not start the commit\n"
"\tmessage editor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--cleanup=<mode>::`\n"
"\tThis option determines how the commit message will be cleaned up before\n"
"\tbeing passed on to the commit machinery. See linkgit:git-commit[1] for "
"more\n"
"\tdetails. In particular, if the '<mode>' is given a value of `scissors`,\n"
"\tscissors will be appended to `MERGE_MSG` before being passed on in the "
"case\n"
"\tof a conflict.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:87
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-n::`\n"
"`--no-commit::`\n"
"\tUsually the command automatically creates some commits with\n"
"\tcommit log messages stating which commits were\n"
"\treverted.  This flag applies the changes necessary\n"
"\tto revert the named commits to your working tree\n"
"\tand the index, but does not make the commits.  In addition,\n"
"\twhen this option is used, your index does not have to match\n"
"\tthe HEAD commit.  The revert is done against the\n"
"\tbeginning state of your index.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:90
msgid ""
"This is useful when reverting more than one commits' effect to your index in "
"a row."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:99
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-S[<keyid>]::`\n"
"`--gpg-sign[=<keyid>]::`\n"
"`--no-gpg-sign::`\n"
"\tGPG-sign commits. The `keyid` argument is optional and\n"
"\tdefaults to the committer identity; if specified, it must be\n"
"\tstuck to the option without a space. `--no-gpg-sign` is useful to\n"
"\tcountermand both `commit.gpgSign` configuration variable, and\n"
"\tearlier `--gpg-sign`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-s::`\n"
"`--signoff::`\n"
"\tAdd a `Signed-off-by` trailer at the end of the commit message.\n"
"\tSee the signoff option in linkgit:git-commit[1] for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--strategy=<strategy>::`\n"
"\tUse the given merge strategy.  Should only be used once.\n"
"\tSee the MERGE STRATEGIES section in linkgit:git-merge[1]\n"
"\tfor details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-X<option>::`\n"
"`--strategy-option=<option>::`\n"
"\tPass the merge strategy-specific option through to the\n"
"\tmerge strategy.  See linkgit:git-merge[1] for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--reference::`\n"
"\tInstead of starting the body of the log message with \"This\n"
"\treverts <full-object-name-of-the-commit-being-reverted>.\",\n"
"\trefer to the commit using \"--pretty=reference\" format\n"
"\t(cf. linkgit:git-log[1]).  The `revert.reference`\n"
"\tconfiguration variable can be used to enable this option by\n"
"\tdefault.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "SEQUENCER SUBCOMMANDS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git revert HEAD~3`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:136
msgid ""
"Revert the changes specified by the fourth last commit in HEAD and create a "
"new commit with the reverted changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git revert -n master~5..master~2`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:144
msgid ""
"Revert the changes done by commits from the fifth last commit in master "
"(included) to the third last commit in master (included), but do not create "
"any commit with the reverted changes. The revert only modifies the working "
"tree and the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:146
#, no-wrap
msgid "DISCUSSION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-revert.txt:154
msgid ""
"While git creates a basic commit message automatically, it is _strongly_ "
"recommended to explain why the original commit is being reverted.  In "
"addition, repeatedly reverting reverts will result in increasingly unwieldy "
"subject lines, for example 'Reapply \"Reapply \"<original-subject>\"\"'.  "
"Please consider rewording these to be shorter and more unique."
msgstr ""

